# esp_board_manager Integration

## Dependency Declaration

In `main/idf_component.yml`, depend on esp_board_manager with an open
version range (`"*"`). No other manual pinning is needed — the component
manager pulls the latest compatible release.

```yaml
dependencies:
  espressif/esp_board_manager: "*"
```

## CMakeLists

Do **NOT** add `esp_board_manager` to `REQUIRES` in `main/CMakeLists.txt`.
The dep declared in `idf_component.yml` is enough; the component manager
wires it into the build and makes the headers visible.

```cmake
idf_component_register(
    SRCS         "app_main.c"
    INCLUDE_DIRS "."
    REQUIRES     fatfs       # only app-domain components here
)
```

## gen_bmgr_codes

`components/gen_bmgr_codes/` is **auto-generated** — never hand-write it
and never copy it from another project.  It is produced when the board
is selected via the board_manager tooling.

The generator writes the board pin map, peripheral config, device list,
and `board_manager.defaults` (which feeds sdkconfig) into
`components/gen_bmgr_codes/`. That component's `CMakeLists.txt` does
`REQUIRES esp_board_manager` + `WHOLE_ARCHIVE TRUE`, so its init code
is pulled into the final binary without the app needing to reference it.

## Build Workflow

The `bmgr` assistant (registered via `IDF_EXTRA_ACTIONS_PATH`) downloads
`esp_board_manager` on first use and selects the target chip from the
chosen board's YAML — **do NOT run `idf.py set-target` manually**.

Standard flow:

```bash
idf.py bmgr -b <board_name>     # e.g. esp32_s3_korvo2_v3 — picks board + target
idf.py build                    # build directly, no set-target needed
```

### Troubleshooting board_manager build errors

When the build complains about `esp_board_manager` / `gen_bmgr_codes`,
follow this order — **never "fix" it by editing `sdkconfig` or adding
explicit deps in `CMakeLists.txt`**:

1. Confirm `esp_board_manager` is actually pulled in
   (`managed_components/espressif__esp_board_manager/` exists after a
   component-manager run).
2. Run `idf.py bmgr -l` to list available boards.
   - If this command itself errors out, `IDF_EXTRA_ACTIONS_PATH` is not
     set / points at the wrong location. Fix the env var first, then
     re-run `idf.py bmgr -b <board_name>` to regenerate
     `components/gen_bmgr_codes/`.
3. If the build still fails, wipe board_manager state with
   `idf.py bmgr -x` (clears every artifact bmgr produced), then redo
   `idf.py bmgr -b <board_name>` and `idf.py build`.

### Hard rule on dependencies

Dependencies on `esp_board_manager` and any board-driven components are
declared **only** via `main/idf_component.yml`. Do not paper over a
board_manager error by hand-adding `REQUIRES esp_board_manager` (or its
sub-components) to `CMakeLists.txt`, and do not hand-set
`CONFIG_ESP_BOARD_*` / legacy audio_board selectors in `sdkconfig`. The
YAML dependency is the single source of truth; everything else is
generated.

## sdkconfig

Do not set legacy audio_board selectors like
`CONFIG_ESP32_S3_KORVO2_V3_BOARD=y` — board selection is owned by the
generated `components/gen_bmgr_codes/board_manager.defaults` (e.g.
`CONFIG_ESP_BOARD_ESP32_S3_KORVO2_V3=y`).

## Single-Device Init (e.g. SD card only)

Use `esp_board_manager_init_device_by_name` to bring up just one device
without running the full `esp_board_manager_init`:

```c
#include "dev_fs_fat.h"
#include "esp_board_device.h"
#include "esp_board_manager.h"
#include "esp_board_manager_defs.h"

esp_err_t ret = esp_board_manager_init_device_by_name(ESP_BOARD_DEVICE_NAME_FS_SDCARD);
if (ret != ESP_OK) { /* handle */ }

dev_fs_fat_handle_t *sd = NULL;
esp_board_device_get_handle(ESP_BOARD_DEVICE_NAME_FS_SDCARD, (void **)&sd);
// Use sd->mount_point instead of hard-coding "/sdcard".

// Teardown:
esp_board_manager_deinit_device_by_name(ESP_BOARD_DEVICE_NAME_FS_SDCARD);
```

Never hand-write `sdmmc_host_t` / `sdmmc_slot_config_t` / `esp_vfs_fat_sdmmc_mount`
in app code when board_manager is in use — the board YAML already owns
pins, slot width, and mount point.
